[37m| Check | Result |[0m
[37m|---|---|[0m
[37m| `python3 -m pip --version` | ✅ pip 24.3.1 (python 3.13) |[0m
[37m| `pip` / `pip3` / `pip3.13` | ✅ all working |[0m
[37m| `import pip` | ✅ |[0m
[37m| Live install (`pip install six`) | ✅ installed + imported |[0m
[37m| `pip check` | ✅ No broken requirements |[0m

[37mI uninstalled the `six` test package afterward, so you're back to a clean state.[0m

[37m## Two things worth flagging[0m

[37m**1. A separate `python3` also lacks pip.** `/usr/bin/python3` is Debian's **3.11.2**, not the same interpreter. It has no pip either, but it's intentionally minimal and flagged `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` (PEP 668), so apt is meant to manage it:[0m
[37m```[0m[37mbash[0m
[37mapt[0m[37m-get[0m[37m install -[0m[37my python3-pip[0m
[37m```[0m
[37mIt's [0m[35m*[0m[35mnot[0m[35m*[0m[37m the[0m[37m default[0m[37m `python3` in your[0m[37m PATH ([0m[32m`[0m[32m/usr/local/bin[0m[32m`[0m[37m wins[0m[37m), so ignore[0m[37m this unless something[0m[37m specifically inv[0m[37mokes [0m[32m`[0m[32m/usr/bin/python3[0m[32m`[0m[37m.[0m

[1;33m**[0m[1;33m2. You're running pip as root into the system interpreter.[0m[1;33m**[0m[37m pip[0m[37m now[0m[37m prints[0m[37m a warning about[0m[37m exactly that, and it's[0m[37m likely[0m[37m how the files got[0m[37m deleted in the first place[0m[37m — a stale/[0m[37mab[0m[37morted install or[0m[37m an over[0m[37mzealous cleanup[0m[37m. For real[0m[37m work, use a venv[0m[37m to[0m[37m avoid[0m[37m re[0m[37m-breaking[0m[37m the system Python[0m[37m:[0m
[37m```[0m[38;5;9mbash[0m
[37mpython3[0m[37m -[0m[37mm[0m[37m venv /app/.venv[0m[37m [0m[37m&&[0m[37m [0m[36msource[0m[37m /app/.venv/bin/activate[0m
